    text q_select "SELECT name, age" #b5482e at.percent [66,86] anchor left font_size 32 hidden true
    text q_from "FROM pets" at.percent [66,78] anchor left font_size 32 hidden true
    text q_where "WHERE species = 'cat'" #2f6b8f at.percent [66,70] anchor left font_size 32 hidden true
    group query cites ["sequel"] members ["q_select","q_from","q_where"] hidden true
    text n3 "3" left q_select gap 18 #8a5fa8 font_size 34 hidden true
    text n1 "1" left q_from gap 18 #8a5fa8 font_size 34 hidden true
    text n2 "2" left q_where gap 18 #8a5fa8 font_size 34 hidden true
    ```python q show none hidden true
    import sqlite3
    species = "cat"
    db = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")
    db.execute("CREATE TABLE pets (name, species, age, owner)")
    db.executemany("INSERT INTO pets VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)", [[\"Rex\", \"dog\", 7, \"Ana\"], [\"Misty\", \"cat\", 3, \"Ben\"], [\"Bella\", \"dog\", 2, \"Cara\"], [\"Tiger\", \"cat\", 9, \"Dev\"], [\"Coco\", \"parrot\", 25, \"Eli\"], [\"Luna\", \"cat\", 1, \"Fay\"]])
    res = db.execute("SELECT name, age FROM pets WHERE species = ?", (species,)).fetchall()
    ans = {"columns": ["name", "age"], "rows": [list(r) for r in res]}
    ```
    text seq "SEQUEL, 1974" #8f887c at.percent [66,60] anchor left font_size 26 hidden true
    text qz1 "SELECT owner" #b5482e at.percent [66,86] anchor left font_size 30 hidden true
    text qz2 "FROM pets" at.percent [66,78] anchor left font_size 30 hidden true
    text qz3 "WHERE age > 5" #2f6b8f at.percent [66,70] anchor left font_size 30 hidden true
    group qz members ["qz1","qz2","qz3"] hidden true

    card title "What is SQL?"

SQL is the language we use to ask a database questions — so how does one question pull out just the (@draw r1_name r1_species r1_age r1_owner r2_name r2_species r2_age r2_owner parallel true@) rows and columns you want?
    draw h_name h_species h_age h_owner parallel true

Take a (@draw r3_name r3_species r3_age r3_owner r4_name r4_species r4_age r4_owner r5_name r5_species r5_age r5_owner r6_name r6_species r6_age r6_owner parallel true@) small table of pets: each row is one animal, each column (@highlight h_name h_species h_age h_owner@) one fact about it.

Say we want the names and ages of the cats, and nothing else.
    highlight r1_species r2_species r3_species r4_species r5_species r6_species

In most programming languages you (@draw loop_box loop_line_1@) would spell out the steps: go through every (@draw loop_line_2@) row, test it, copy the (@draw loop_line_3@) parts you need.

SQL skips the steps: you describe the (@draw q_select q_from q_where@) result in a query, and the database works out how to get it.
    erase loop

This way of asking is older than it (@draw seq@) looks: IBM researchers designed SQL in the early 1970s, first calling it SEQUEL.

    erase seq

FROM pets says which (@highlight tbl effect box@) table to look in.
    highlight q_from

Then WHERE species equals cat sets a test that every row must pass.
    highlight q_where

The database checks each (@fade r1_name r1_species r1_age r1_owner to 0.2@) row in turn: Rex is a dog, so out; (@highlight r2_name r2_species r2_age r2_owner@) Misty is a cat, so in.

Three cats pass, the other three rows drop out: WHERE picks the rows.
    fade r3_name r3_species r3_age r3_owner r5_name r5_species r5_age r5_owner to 0.2

Now the columns: SELECT name, age says which ones to keep.
    highlight q_select

Species and owner fall away, and what is left is our answer.
    fade h_species r1_species r2_species r3_species r4_species r5_species r6_species h_owner r1_owner r2_owner r3_owner r4_owner r5_owner r6_owner to 0.2

The answer comes back as a table of its own: three names, three ages.
    draw header row_1 row_2 row_3

Read the query again, though: (@draw n3@) you write SELECT first, but it acts last.

In its logic, the (@draw n1@) database starts at FROM, filters (@draw n2@) with WHERE, and only then picks (@highlight n3@) the columns.

That order is the whole (@highlight q_where@) idea: choose the rows, then (@highlight q_select@) the columns, and say what you want, never how.
    fade h_name h_species h_age h_owner r1_name r1_species r1_age r1_owner r2_name r2_species r2_age r2_owner r3_name r3_species r3_age r3_owner r4_name r4_species r4_age r4_owner r5_name r5_species r5_age r5_owner r6_name r6_species r6_age r6_owner to 1

    erase header row_1 row_2 row_3 n1 n2 n3 q_select q_from q_where parallel true

One last check, on the same table: what does this query give back?
    draw qz1 qz2 qz3

    quiz "Same table. What does SELECT owner FROM pets WHERE age > 5 give back?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 805 buttons_at.y 348 buttons_layout column
        right: Ana, Dev and Eli: WHERE keeps Rex, Tiger and Coco, and SELECT keeps only their owner column.
        wrong: Do it in the database's order: which pets are older than five, and then which column does SELECT keep?
        + Ana, Dev, Eli
        * Three whole rows
        * All six owners

So every question you ask a database comes down to (@highlight r1_name r1_species r1_age r1_owner r4_name r4_species r4_age r4_owner r5_name r5_species r5_age r5_owner@) two choices: which rows, and (@highlight r1_owner r4_owner r5_owner effect circle@) which columns.

